view: a program and its view, and the messages between them

A program says what to show as nodes; a view draws them and hands back
what the person did. fm does what a file manager does and ed what an
editor does; how either is shown is the view's: the terminal on tty
(`view/term.go`), gio's window (`gio/README`), a page next. Nodes, not
cells: cells would make the page a terminal in a browser, as ttyd does,
where nodes let a view draw a pill or a chip.

    ```
    type View interface {
        Draw(n *Node, status string, state []string)
        Key() (Key, bool)
        Ask(prompt, def string) (string, bool)
        Size() (w, h int)
        Copy(text string) error
    }
    ```

Messages, not calls
  A program and its view pass only plain data: in one process a call
  whose arguments are data, down a pipe or a socket a JSON line each
  (`outline/outline.go:/msg is one line on the wire/`).
    to the view      draw: the tree, the program's word and its state;
                     ask: a prompt; copy: text for the clipboard
    to the program   key: its name, the node it was said on, the rune a
                     click fell on, any text; answer: the line typed;
                     size: how much the view shows
  A message joins when a view needs one: help is nodes a view lays out
  (`outline/help.go:/func Help/`). What a program runs is no message: it
  runs on a terminal, the program's console, below.

Who holds what
  program  the data, the cursor and the selection: what its verbs act on.
           fm sends the folder it stands in, ed every line, its cursor
           and its selection as runs.
  view     how it shows: a folder as rows or as thumbs, t turning between
           them, and an outline's parts folded or not. It moves the cursor
           among what it shows, and tells the program where it landed;
           going in and out of a folder is the program's, for it changes
           what is shown.
  between  ideas, by name: down, in, rename. A key is one way to say one
           and a click another; ed passes its keys through, for an
           editor's keys are its language.

The console: the terminal what a program runs runs on
  What a program runs needs a terminal: fzy for a pick, v, a pause. A
  program is given one as unix gives one, by the host that starts it on
  it, and what it runs finds it at /dev/tty, as fzy does. Drawn on a
  terminal, as in a tmux pane, the program steps aside on it for the
  child's time. Down a pipe, its view elsewhere, it has the console its
  host gives it, its error and controlling terminal: gio's frame starts
  each slot's program on a pty of its own, behind its view, as tmux
  starts each pane's (`gio/host.go:/launch starts what runs in a pane/`).
  A program sent a key that has not drawn since is acting, and what
  writes on its console then is what it runs: the slot shows the
  console, the keys going to it, until the program draws again. o and e
  write nothing, so they show nothing. A program given none, fm -w,
  whose window has none to give, or a test, says so.

The status line, at the bottom only, in every view
  The program's word on the left, what just happened or what it asks; on
  the right its state, [2] selected, cut:3, mark:x, then the view's, its
  layout and where the cursor is. ed says its mode, + while unsaved,
  held while that keeps it from cur's file, and line:col. A prompt takes
  the line while it asks.

Kinds, never looks
  A node says what it is, its kind, and never how it looks, which is the
  view's: troff's way, one stream and a device each. A reader makes the
  tree of a thing, the right tool for its kind
  (`design/lens:/The reader, by kind/`), and each view draws a kind its
  best: the terminal in runes, as nroff does, gio's window in pixels, as
  troff does, and the page in html. A kind a view does not know it draws
  as its text, so a kind is a promise of more, which a view keeps if it
  can, and no view says what it can do. The kinds are few, and how each
  looks is the view's own table, which no program sends
  (`view/term.go:/var looks/`):
	a note's     title section para row item step code (outline.Parts)
	a program's  type func value (outline.Go)
	a run's      addr, an address; name, what a declaration names; and
	             the text around them: its part's runs, not its parts
	the floor's  line, a line by its indent (outline.Text)
	a folder's   dir file link exec bad, fm's things
	a diff's     add del same hunk meta, and waits, a lens's why
	a tree's     outline doc picture: rows that fold, a page set for
	             the eye, parts and messages laid out
  What is heavy goes by its name, an image by its path, for the view to
  open; and a figure, whose arrangement is its meaning, is laid out once,
  in cells, as sys/picture lays one out, each view drawing the cells its
  way. A picture is the one tree a view reads, not draws only: its parts
  go as the note's lines, which sys/picture, asked by kind, reads. The
  terminal's best is nroff's: a doc's prose to a measure, a name bold, an
  address underlined, a top head ruled off, and a declaration, which is
  code, on the panel, its doc, which is prose, hung under it; and an
  outline's rows its parts' names, a quiet guide under each part unfolded,
  what is said of a part on the cursor's row alone. Both set parts apart
  as one rule says: a blank line between parts of two kinds and around a
  part that holds parts, while leaves of a kind stand together, as a table
  does.

The structure is the tree, not a language
  Folders and files carry it down to the idea, and inside a file the
  note's own marks carry the rest: its indent, a bullet, two spaces, a
  fence, which a writer makes anyway, and which its reader reads. A
  primitive is a kind the views know, a package of its own as
  sys/picture is, asked by kind: no plugin system and no runtime library.
  fm and ed stay programs, each at the end of messages; as Go packages
  one process could host both, which stays open.
